{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>When there is among you an evyon, etc.</b> Its saying <b>bekha</b> — perhaps it intends to allude to the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Bava Batra 10a) who said that the reason HaShem chooses to chastise a person in this world with the sufferings of poverty is so that through him his fellow may merit [the reward of] sustaining him. And this is what it alluded to in the phrase <b>bekha evyon</b> — meaning, because of you the great poverty came upon him. And its saying <b>me'echad akhekha</b> — meaning, do not think when you see him an evyon that he is lesser; know that he is among the select of your brethren, and poverty is no proof that he is lowly among brethren. And the reason it arranged these gradations — <b>me'echad akhekha … me'artsekha</b> — even though our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 71a) that this comes to say: your poor and your city's poor [take precedence], etc. —" "<b>Further,</b> it is also possible to say [that it concerns] the gradations of the decrees that HaShem decrees upon the evyonim. I have already explained in the verse (Exodus 22:24) ״אִם כֶּסֶף תַּלְוֶה״ etc., that the poor man's portion HaShem deposits in the hand of the rich man, and this is his wealth — the poor people's money is deposited in his hand. Now sometimes HaShem deposits this poor man's portion with a person close to the [poor] man, and he will not strain to take his livelihood from him since he is his relative, nor will he need to travel the roads. But when HaShem judges with righteousness to afflict the poor man, He distances his livelihood to a stranger and even to a distant place, and the evyon strains to wander from city to city to find the place where HaShem has prepared his soul's sustenance. And this is its saying <b>me'echad akhekha</b>, or even if he is not your brother but he is in one of your gates, or even if he is not of your city in your land." "<b>Or</b> it may intend its saying <b>be'artsekha</b> — meaning, even in your land, which is a place of wealth for all Israel, even so an evyon may be found there." "<b>And by way of</b> remez it hints a great allusion concerning one of the select of the nation, toward whom we direct our hopes and await his coming — and he is the King of Israel, our Mashiach, who is an evyon. He was already likened to a poor man, as it says (Zechariah 9:9) ״עָנִי וְרוֹכֵב עַל חֲמוֹר״, and it alluded to the matter that surrounds him to make him an evyon, saying <b>bekha</b> — meaning, because of you, for our sins prolonged our end. It also hinted in the word <b>bekha</b> that he is an evyon with us, longing when he will come to redeem us. Go forth and learn from what our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the story of Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, that when he saw King Mashiach he asked him about Israel in the world, and Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi told him that they are sitting and awaiting his coming; and when he heard this, the pious one sobbed in great weeping out of his longing to come and redeem them. And therefore it alluded by saying <b>me'echad akhekha</b> — he is the select one among your brethren, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory explained (Targum Yonatan on Genesis 26:10) in the verse ״כִּמְעַט שָׁכַב אַחַד הָעָם״, which means the select one who is the king of the people. And its saying <b>be'achad she'arekha</b> alludes to him that he is the select one of the Sanhedrin, none like him as a decisor, as it says (Isaiah 11:3–4) ״וְשָׁפַט בְּצֶדֶק״ etc. ״וְלֹא לְמַרְאֵה עֵינָיו יִשְׁפּוֹט״ etc., but rather ״וַהֲרִיחוֹ בְּיִרְאַת ה׳״. And the meaning of she'arekha is along the lines of the verse (Deuteronomy 25:7) ״וְעָלְתָה יְבִמְתּוֹ הַשַּׁעְרָה״. And its saying <b>be'artsekha</b> alludes that his conception and birth were in the holy Land of Israel." "<b>Or</b> it may intend along this path: <b>evyon be'achad she'arekha</b>, according to their words of blessed memory in the Book of the Zohar (Zohar Ḥadash, Parashat Noaḥ), and this is its language: אִי חֲדָא קְהִילָא אוֹ חֲדָא כְּנִישְׁתָּא יִתְּעָרוּן בִּתְיוּבְתָּא מִיָּד אָתֵי בֶּן דָּוִד — end quote. Hence evyon means ta'av (longing) for this thing — namely, one of your gates that would suffice to fulfill his desire to come with joy. And its saying <b>be'artsekha</b> alludes that the place of his longing is the Holy Land which HaShem your Elohim gives you, which he longs for — when will the oppressing city (Zephaniah 1:1) be remembered, the desire of hearts. And HaShem commands every man of Israel not to harden his heart etc., but to strengthen himself with all might and power to fulfill the desire of the one select one, for through the deeds of human beings — and in particular the mitzvah of charity, as it is written (Isaiah 54:14) ״בִּצְדָקָה תִּכּוֹנָנִי״. And it concludes by saying <b>me'akhekha ha'evyon</b> — meaning, for the sake of your brother the known evyon — that a person should set in his mind that the deed is for the purpose of this matter of HaShem's Mashiach, whose name is Ḥayyim."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The comment ends with an open citation of Bava Metzi'a 71a; the clause appears deliberately trailing into the next comment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:51.440841675Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}